# Code and Git Conventions

- Name new branches `YYMMDD-Feature-description`, using the creation date,
  literal `Feature`, and a concise English kebab-case description.
- Example: `261124-Feature-model-training-notes`.
- Follow the applicable Google language style guide for new or modified code;
  use the Google Python Style Guide for Python and notebook code.
- Keep changes focused; do not reformat unrelated code or rewrite the user's prose.
- Place explanatory code comments above the corresponding code, not at line end.
- Before committing, inspect status, staged and unstaged diffs, and recent history.
- Use concise imperative commit subjects explaining the purpose of the change;
  preserve the repository's existing `docs:`, `fix:`, or `feat:` convention.
- Commit and push only when explicitly requested; stage only authorized files.
- Never commit credentials, local `.env`, model weights, or unrelated local scripts.
- Do not merge into the default branch, force-push, or rewrite published commits
  without explicit permission. Let the user decide whether to merge a review branch.
- Preserve unrelated local changes; ask before discarding or overwriting them.
